Let's begin with something that feels almost science fiction practical.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.840
Engineers have built a memory chip that keeps working at 700 degrees Celsius,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.740
hot enough to melt lead and far beyond what any conventional silicon device can tolerate.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:31.380
The team stacked unusual combinations of materials to create a device that doesn't

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.440
just store data under extreme heat, but can actually perform calculations while it's doing so.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.240
What makes the work especially interesting is that the key heat-resistant mechanism

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.600
at the atomic level was partly uncovered by accident during routine testing.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.960
The researchers noticed the stack kept functioning long after they expected it to fail.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.920
This isn't simply another incremental improvement in thermal tolerance.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.020
It's a demonstration that computation itself can be made compatible with environments we

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:05.280
used to think would destroy any electronics. The implications stretch across several frontiers.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.960
Imagine reliable AI hardware inside aerospace vehicles that have to survive reentry temperatures,

